# The Inkstone reader, on a server.  Copy to `.env` at the REPO ROOT and fill in.
#
#   cp tools/web/env.example .env
#
# ⚠️ The three SECRETS are NOT here. They go in files under `secrets/`, because an environment
# variable is readable through `docker inspect`, through `/proc/<pid>/environ`, and by every
# child process — which is why Docker's own guidance is to prefer secrets for credentials.
#
#   mkdir -p secrets
#
#   The Nextcloud credential is easiest to get by signing in, which writes the file for you
#   and never asks you for a password:
#
#       py tools/web/server.py --login --secrets-dir secrets
#
#   Or paste an app password you minted by hand:
#   printf '%s' 'xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx' > secrets/nc_password
#   printf '%s' 'a passphrase you will remember' > secrets/web_password
#   openssl rand -hex 32                        > secrets/mcp_token
#   chmod 600 secrets/*
#
# `printf` rather than `echo`: echo appends a newline, and a trailing newline in a password is
# invisible, survives every inspection, and fails every comparison. (The reader strips it anyway
# — but the habit is worth more than the safety net.)
#
# `secrets/` must be gitignored. It is.

# Your Nextcloud. Scheme and host only, https:// required.
INKSTONE_NC_URL=https://nextcloud.example.com

# Your uid, which is not always your login name. The reader prints the one the server reports.
INKSTONE_NC_UID=yourname

# The public name(s) this reader is served as, comma separated. REQUIRED off loopback: it is the
# DNS-rebinding check, and a hostile page pointing its own name at your server is what it stops.
INKSTONE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=notes.example.com
